/// Parallel — block-based fan-out/join combinators (no awaitable futures).
///
/// Beamtalk sends are synchronous by default (ADR 0104): a `.` send blocks
/// the caller and types as the method's return value. The `Future` stub was
/// deliberately removed (BT-1057) to avoid reintroducing async/await
/// "function coloring". `Parallel` gives that same blocking contract to
/// *fan-out* work: each block runs in its own spawned process, but `all:`/
/// `any:` themselves block the caller and return plain `Result` values —
/// no future/promise handle ever escapes into user code.
///
/// A dedicated class (rather than adding these as `Block`/`Collection`
/// methods) was chosen because these combinators operate over a *collection
/// of blocks*, not a single block or a single collection of data — `Parallel`
/// names that operation the way `OS`/`Random`/`System` name other class-side
/// utility namespaces. `Collection>>parallelCollect:` (see `collection.bt`)
/// is the ergonomic "map my elements in parallel" convenience built on top
/// of `Parallel all:`.
///
/// ## Examples
/// ```beamtalk
/// (Parallel all: #([1 + 1], [2 + 2])) collect: [:r | r value]
///   // => #(2, 4)
///
/// results := Parallel all: #([1 / 0], [42])
/// (results at: 1) isError    // => true
/// (results at: 2) value      // => 42
///
/// (Parallel any: #([1], [2])) isOk   // => true
/// ```
///
/// ## Process model
/// Each block is spawned in its own process, both linked and monitored to
/// the caller: the link guarantees a worker cannot outlive the caller (no
/// orphans if the caller dies mid-flight); the monitor lets the caller
/// observe each worker's completion without a worker's exit being able to
/// kill it in return. Every block runs inside an exception boundary, so a
/// raised exception — or a non-local return (`^`) that can no longer reach
/// its enclosing method once running in another process — always becomes a
/// `Result error:` for that slot rather than crashing the caller. See
/// `beamtalk_parallel.erl` for the full process-model writeup.
sealed typed Object subclass: Parallel native: beamtalk_parallel

  /// Run each zero-argument block in `blocks` in its own process; block the
  /// caller until every block finishes. Returns one `Result` per block, in
  /// the same order as `blocks` — a block that raises yields `Result
  /// error:` for its slot, but the other blocks still run to completion.
  ///
  /// ## Examples
  /// ```beamtalk
  /// (Parallel all: #([1 + 1], [2 + 2])) collect: [:r | r value]  // => #(2, 4)
  /// ((Parallel all: #([1 / 0], [42])) at: 1) isError             // => true
  /// Parallel all: #()                                            // => #()
  /// ```
  class sealed all: blocks :: List(Block) -> List(Result) => self delegate

  /// Like `all:`, but with an overall wall-clock deadline (Integer
  /// milliseconds or a `Duration`), measured from when `all:timeout:` is
  /// sent — not per block.
  ///
  /// On timeout, every block still running is killed and its slot becomes
  /// `Result error:` with a `#timeout` error; blocks that already finished
  /// keep their real result.
  ///
  /// ## Examples
  /// ```beamtalk
  /// results := Parallel all: #([Timer sleep: 2000. 1], [2]) timeout: 50
  /// (results at: 1) isError    // => true
  /// (results at: 2) value      // => 2
  /// ```
  class sealed all: blocks :: List(Block) timeout: ms :: Integer | Duration -> List(Result) =>
    self delegate

  /// Run each zero-argument block in `blocks` in its own process; return the
  /// first successful `Result`. Every still-running block is killed as soon
  /// as a winner is found. If every block fails, returns `Result error:`
  /// wrapping a `List` of the individual failure reasons, in input order.
  ///
  /// Raises `#type_error` if `blocks` is empty.
  ///
  /// **No overall timeout**: unlike `all:timeout:`, there is no `any:timeout:`
  /// today. If every block hangs (blocked I/O, an infinite loop, a deadlock),
  /// this call blocks its caller forever — there is no escape. Give every
  /// block its own internal deadline (e.g. via `Timer`) if it calls into
  /// anything that might not return.
  ///
  /// ## Examples
  /// ```beamtalk
  /// (Parallel any: #([1], [2])) isOk           // => true
  /// (Parallel any: #([1 / 0], [2 / 0])) isError // => true
  /// ```
  class sealed any: blocks :: List(Block) -> Result => self delegate
